+## 2. What the source actually says
+
+### 2.1 Splitting data before descent
+
+**SOURCE-READ (p. 164).** Distinct roots of a leading coefficient polynomial
+correspond to distinct child subdiscs. A factor's multiplicity records the
+number of roots in the associated child, after the degree-proportional scaling
+for the polynomial being considered.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (Prop. 4.6, pp. 170–171).** For `r>=2`, the relevant leading
+coefficients are powers of a common polynomial `p(pi)` of degree `nu`. There is
+a polynomial `q(pi)` of degree
+
+```text
+nu (n-M_r) / d_r
+```
+
+which is square-free, contains every root of `p`, and for which `p` is not a
+power of `q`. This bounds the number of distinct `p`-roots; it does not specify
+their multiplicities or constants. Moh's following remark extends the
+proposition to the monomial-Jacobian form by replacing its order hypothesis.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (p. 173).** Moh describes Proposition 4.6 as the splitting
+phenomenon: it determines the number of child discs through `deg q` and says
+their root numbers are unequal in the situation discussed there. It does not
+provide the whole factorisation of `p`.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (Def. 5.1, p. 179).** The tower datum specifies the number of
+roots in each *chosen major disc*, gives inequalities for its multiplicity, and
+computes the major radii `delta_i` by an explicit formula. It is a major-tower
+datum, not the complete sibling tree.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (pp. 190–191).** A `p`-factor of multiplicity `E` continues a
+major tower when `E>d_r/(n-M_r)`; a factor with
+`1<=E<=d_r/(n-M_r)` gives the minor disc constructed in section 6. Proposition
+6.1 defines that minor radius from the actual minimum of pairwise root orders
+and proves only `delta*_{r-1}>=1`.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (pp. 193–194).** Moh explicitly says Proposition 6.1 does not
+specify `delta*`; it only estimates it. He calls the uncertainty intrinsic and
+explains that changing constants or quasi-approximate roots changes the leading
+coefficients. Thus neither Def. 5.1 nor Props. 4.4/4.6 recover the missing
+minor radii or residue constants from `(M,V)`.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (p. 194).** In the original `M_s=n-2` case the highest form is
+
+```text
+[(y-a*x)^v_s (y-b*x)^u_s]^(n/d_s),       u_s=d_s-v_s, a!=b.
+```
+
+This fixes the original two-factor top split. It does not say that the pair
+obtained by focusing on the original minor branch has the same two factors.
+
+### 2.2 Propositions 6.2–6.4
+
+**SOURCE-READ (Prop. 6.2, p. 195).** After `z=y-bx-e`, the `y`- and `z`-degree
+parts are the contributions proportional to `v_s/d_s` and `u_s/d_s`. This is
+the degree reduction used by the descent.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (Lemma 6.2, pp. 196–197).** The inversion lemma says suitable
+truncations in the old and new parameters determine one another and identifies
+their Puiseux fields. It does not assert a factor partition.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (Prop. 6.3, pp. 197–198).** Assuming `delta_s=-1` and
+`delta*_{s-1}>=v_s/u_s`, Moh constructs a truncated `pi`-root and a parameter
+`gamma`. The transformed expressions lie in `k[gamma,pi]`, are monic in `pi`,
+and have `pi`-degrees
+
+```text
+u_s*n/d_s,  u_s*(-mu_1)/d_s, ..., u_s*(-mu_{s-1})/d_s.
+```
+
+Their Jacobian is a nonzero scalar times `gamma^(v_s-u_s-1)`. The proposition
+contains no statement fixing a descended leading factorisation, number of
+minor children, exact minor radius, or residue constants.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (Prop. 6.4, pp. 198–199).** When `u_s=1`, Moh proves
+`delta*_{s-1}>=v_s/u_s=v_s`; hence Prop. 6.3 applies. This remains an
+inequality, not an equality or a split theorem.
+
+**SOURCE-READ (p. 209).** In the fourth Appendix-II case Moh explicitly treats
+two possibilities according to the distribution of roots inside a minor disc.
+This is direct evidence that the distribution is extra data.
+
+### 2.3 The transformed table
+
+**SOURCE-READ (p. 207).** The transformed columns and linked alternatives are:
+
+| `n` | `m=-M1` | `M2` | `V2` | `delta2` | `delta1` | Jacobian |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| 16 | 12 | 13 | 3 | -1 | 1/4 | `X` |
+| 21 | 14 | 16 `[18]` | 2 `[5]` | -1/2 `[-1]` | 7/6 `[1/3]` | `X` |
+| 15 | 10 | 11 | 3 `[2]` | -1 | 1/2 `[4/3]` | `X^2` |
+
+**SOURCE-READ.** Moh applies Props. 6.4 and 6.3 because `u_3=1` in the first
+three original cases and reports reduced raw coefficient counts `244,373,202`.
+The bracketed entries in a row are linked alternatives, not independent
+columns to mix.
+
+## 3. The safe general rule—and where it stops
+
+**DERIVED from Prop. 6.3 and the charged drop rule.** For an original skeleton
+with `u_s=1`, first form `n'=n/d_s`, `m'=m/d_s`, and
+`k=V_s-u_s-1=V_s-2`; divide retained `M` entries by `d_s`, remove any
+`M'=n'-1` level, and carry its surviving `V` path to `(M2',V2')`. The nine
+instances are checked explicitly in section 5. The original singleton
+`u_s=1` enables this descent; it is not a theorem that one of the later
+descended multiplicities `e_i` is one.
+
+Let the descended data be `(n',m';M2',V2';k)` and put
+
+```text
+K=d2'=gcd(n',m'),  u'=K-V2',  d'=m'/K,  e'=n'/K.
+```
+
+**DERIVED.** At final level `r=2`, Def. 5.1 has `V3=d3`; hence the common
+boundary polynomial `p` has degree `K`. The selected major factor accounts for
+`V2'`, so all other `p`-factor multiplicities form a partition
+`lambda=(e_1,...,e_l)` with `sum e_i=u'`.
+
+**DERIVED.** Proposition 4.6 gives `deg q=n'-M2'` at this level. Therefore
+
+```text
+1+l <= n'-M2'.
+```
+
+The extra `1` is the selected `V2'` factor. Its condition that `p` not be a
+power of `q` must also be retained; none of the 30 strata in section 5 violates
+it.
+
+**DERIVED, conditional on actual boundary data.** If the distinct noncentre
+residue constants are `a_i`, then
+
+```text
+H_lambda(x,y) = y^V2' product_i (y-a_i*x)^e_i,
+f_top = H_lambda^d',                 g_top = H_lambda^e'.
+```
+
+Thus the number of noncentre roots of `H` is exactly `u'` with multiplicity,
+while the corresponding root packets contain `d'e_i` roots of `f` and
+`e'e_i` roots of `g`. Distinct child discs separate at the known top radius
+`delta2'=-1`; radii and splits *inside* a repeated `e_i` block are not known.
+
+**DERIVED.** The monomial Jacobian marks the line `x=0`. Coordinate changes
+that retain `J=c*x^k` can put the selected major slope at zero and one nonzero
+slope at one, but they do not remove all remaining slopes. For
+`lambda=(e_1,...,e_l)` use the source-complete family
+
+```text
+H_V[e_1,...,e_l]
+ = y^V (y-x)^e_1 product_(i=2..l) (y-a_i*x)^e_i,
+
+Omega = product_(i>=2) a_i(a_i-1) product_(2<=i<j) (a_i-a_j) != 0.
+```
+
+There are `l-1` slope parameters. Fixing them numerically is a slice unless a
+separate source statement fixes them. The Singular charts saturate by
+`c*Omega`, not merely by `c`.
+
+**OPEN[INVERSE-MINOR].** Moh's one example says its inverse transformation
+forces a minor disc. If this were proved generally, at least one part would
+satisfy
+
+```text
+e_i <= floor(K/(n'-M2')).
+```
+
+That is an additional necessary condition, not a sourced theorem for all
+descendants. It selects 21 of the 30 source-safe overcases; it is never used to
+discard one of the other nine in this report.
+
+## 4. Moh controls
+
+### 4.1 Why `2,2,6` means `y^3(y^2-x^2)`
+
+**SOURCE-READ (pp. 210–211).** Moh is considering the transform of degrees
+`(75,50)` with descended data `(15,10;M2=11,V2=3)` and writes that the inverse
+transformation supplies a minor disc. He concludes that the three children in
+`D2` contain `2,2,6` roots of `f`; equation (5) then has
+
+```text
+h_top = y^3(y^2-x^2) = y^3(y-x)(y+x),    f=h^2+2 beta.
+```
+
+**DERIVED.** Here `K=5`, `deg q=15-11=4`, and the chosen major multiplicity is
+3, leaving mass 2. A minor factor must have integer multiplicity
+`E<=5/4`, hence `E=1`; the remaining unit is another simple factor. Since
+`d'=10/5=2`, the `h` multiplicities `(3,1,1)` become `f`-root packet sizes
+`(6,2,2)`. Moh lists them in the order `2,2,6`. Likewise `g_top=h_top^3`
+has packets `(9,3,3)`.
+
+**SOURCE-READ / DERIVED.** Moh obtains the packet counts before invoking
+`delta1=1/2` for the lower forms of `h` and `beta`. The `+/-x` presentation is
+part of this special normal form. The page does not license copying it to an
+arbitrary `u'=2` row.
+
+### 4.2 The single-root control and K=16, t=2
+
+**SOURCE-READ (p. 208).** For `(16,12;13;3;k=1)`, Moh writes
+
+```text
+h = y^3(y-x) + lower terms.
+```
+
+**DERIVED.** `K=4` and `V2=3`, so `u'=1`: there is only one noncentre root
+unit. Here `d'=3,e'=4`, hence the `f` packets are `(9,3)` and the `g` packets
+are `(12,4)`.
+
+**DERIVED.** The K=16 `t=2` source skeleton
+`(112,80;M=[-80,100,110],V={V2=3,V3=3})` descends to
+`(28,20;25;3;k=1)`. Again `K=4,u'=1`, so its unique normalized face is
+`y^3(y-x)`.
+
+**MEASURED (frozen charged result).** That K=16 `t=2` chart has 27 unknowns
+and 37 equations; it returned saturated `[1]` over the three charged primes and
+over `Q` (exact time 32.440 s). This checks the one-root rule, not a general
+multi-root split.
